A||||15||Fierodave2001||Guess who might not make it to Carlisle ?||||11||
Z||000000||Fierodave2001||05-16-2001||10:21 PM||||Gee,  Guess who might not be able to make it ??   <BR>.<BR>Me,  Fierodave2001  !<BR>.<BR>I have the Fiero up on the trailer, and have tried it out tonight.<BR>.<BR>Around 55 - 60 MPH, the damm trailer starts to sway back & forth  - Scaring the S__t out of me.<BR>.<BR>I've tried slowing down, waiting for the swaying to stop.  Tried applying the brakes, as the trailer has brakes.<BR>.<BR>At one time, and 18 wheeler flew by 'bout 80, and I thought I was going to lose it.<BR>.<BR>I've just about squeezed the steering wheel in two held with a "death grip"<BR>.<BR>Anyone experienced this swaying while towing ?  Holy cow !||||reg||11
Z||000001||danny boy||05-16-2001||10:26 PM|||| In my big truck days, I saw this a lot. Scared the crap outta me. Once I had a duallie pulling some kind of carnival trailer almost take me out on I80 in PA as I tried to pass him.||||reg||1
Z||000002||skitime||05-16-2001||10:47 PM||||How did you put the car on the trialer - foward or backwards.  Most trailers are designed to have the weight at the front of the trailer.  Therefore you would put the car on backwards. Remember the Fieros weight distribution is reversed compared to most cars.  If most of the weight is at the back of the trailer it will do what you described.  Try it!||||reg||1
Z||000003||PBJ||05-16-2001||10:56 PM||||I agree. tounge weight should be at least 10% of total weight. I would go more, maybe 15% if you have a good trailer and truck hitch.  Also tire pressure on trailer and especially on tow vehicle-pump them up.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Archie||05-16-2001||11:35 PM||||I agree, put the car on the trailer backward, then if you still have the condition then you better stay home & we don't want that.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Archie<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hank is Here||05-16-2001||11:51 PM||||Also make sure that the tounge is level.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fierodave2001||05-16-2001||11:57 PM||||When we first put it on, forward, all the  way up to the straps, -  well the Cherokee <BR>looks like a dog squatting for a crap !<P>By that I mean it squatted in the back, & raised the front up.<P>We back it up about a foot and it looked  better. Put own straps & chains on.<P>Now, I understand the one post about backing  it up on the trailer, but again that would make it to heavy on the truck.<P>Yes it's a good "hide away" class 4 hitch rated to 5000 lbs bolted to the frame.<P>Checked the trailer tires, they are 65 lbs as rated.  Will check the truck tires, they<BR>are rated for 35 lbs.<P>In the morning, I'll try and bring it forward about 1/2 foot & see it that helps.<P>If so I'm coming, if not, well I may just rent a truck for the trip. We will see.<BR>Cross that bridge when I get to it.<P>Man, hated that feeling !||||reg||4
Z||000007||007DOUG||05-17-2001||12:10 AM||||Is the cherokee designed to tow? Heavier shocks? swaybars added? proper hitch? <P>Is the trailer designed for a car? <P>I use a properly set-up 99 suburban, and a 20'L x 108"W flatbed trailer. I can easily drive 100mph (only when needed  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">) with no problems. I drive the fiero onto the trailer. Not backwards.<P>------------------<BR>4 Indy pace Cars<BR>84 T-top<BR>2 86 Red GTs (twins) <BR>86 GT Yellow ASAP<BR>86 GT Blue w/4:10 tranny<BR>86 GT Pearl White 3.4 T-top<BR>88 Faded red Formula 5 speed<BR>2 Black 88 GT's (twins)<P>However......<BR><I>"Time is my most precious and most perishable of all my possessions."</I>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fierodave2001||05-17-2001||12:56 AM||||It's rated @ 5000lbs towing pwr w/4.0 liter<P>No, it only has the "stock" shocks.  Had it been ordered w/towing pkg, that w/h come.<P>I had the Reese type hitch type 3 install & trans cooler.  We had this topic covered in  eariler post last week.<P>The U-haul trailer is 2000 lbs, Fiero is around 2700, so it's near it's limit.<P>Of course, many people don't worry about such things, but I want to be carefull.||||reg||6
Z||000009||87GTZ34||05-17-2001||01:30 AM||||Did you try going faster than 60mph.  That could very well be your problem.  The Fiero is get antsy and wants to go faster. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||Taijiguy||05-17-2001||07:40 AM||||When I was doing contracting I used to have that same problem when I would carry materials that were too long for my trailer. It's possible you can pull the car up far enough to just get the tow vehicle to settle a little in the back, but not having a heavy duty suspension, you might be screwed. You could possibly add a set of helper springs to the tow vehicle until something more permenant can be done. <BR>That is a dangerous condition you described. I had a load of pressure treated lumber about put my old Blazer ass-end-to once when the trailer started wandering like you described. Very bad. And applying the brakes is even worse, even if the trailer is equipped with a surge brake, it won't engage, and will just push you right around.<BR>Good luck!<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000011||Fierodave2001||05-17-2001||09:24 AM||||No didn't go over 60.  Going out now to try it again.  Moved it forward to get more weight up front.<P>May go & get dolly.  It's a 5 sp, tow forwards, or backwards on those ?<P>Looking at stock GT low front end worried about that.||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroHarry||05-17-2001||09:29 AM||||Tow with front tires on dolly, you cant hurt the manual tranny unless you leave it in gear and drive off.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR><B>Harry Waggoner</B><BR>1985 GT 263K+ <I>"TEMPTATION"</I><BR><B>"Im not driving to fast, Im flying to low!"</B>||||reg||4
Z||000013||elfiero||05-17-2001||11:41 AM||||Did'nt we JUST have this discussion about the<BR>fact that a cherokee WILL NOT pull this load?<BR>toldya so! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000014||pontiacs4me||05-17-2001||12:58 PM||||My moms boyfriend has a cherokee, and we use it to pull our ski boat to the lake.  With stock shocks, it rubbed, squatted and drove like crap.  He put some heavy duty air shocks in the back and it fixed all the problems.  Then you can adjust the ride height to how you like it, and they only cost like 50 bucks for both.||||reg||1
Z||000015||maryjane||05-17-2001||08:12 PM||||Years ago my boss (not anymore) told me I had to pull a little mud pump to a drilling location on a little tandem lowboy with my El Comino. I told him I wouldn't be able to stop with it or drive in rain but said "go ahead, it'll be fine". It started swayin around just like you describe, usually going downhill. Finally it got too far over to the left and me,El Comino and trailer head off thru the pine trees. When I got stopped, the trailer was sitting outside my passenger door. Scared the **** out of me and it was a while before I would pull anything again. Way too light a vehicle. It just aint worth it.<p>[This message has been edited by maryjane (edited 05-17-2001).]||||reg||4
